; ct.rules — the derived state category, given its own vocabulary.
;
; A model's meaning is a category (kernel Appendix I.1): objects are reachable
; situations, morphisms are move sequences. The four `writ check` modalities
; are the common questions (extension §2); this file names the category's
; structure so a model can be asked the rest.
;
; It is a rules library: load it from a .rules file. It reserves ten relation
; names: `reach`, `mutual`, `recurrent`, `before`, `phase-before`, `one-way`,
; `escapes`, `final-phase`, `moves` and `dead-end`.
;
; Cost: `phase` and `phase-step` are built in and linear, and so is
; everything derived from them. `reach` and `before` answer with sets of pairs,
; quadratic in the situation count. Prefer a linear question; §7's goal forms
; are.

; 1. Hom-sets — the free category on the reachable graph
;
; `reach S T` holds when some move sequence runs from S to T: identity, then
; composition. No `init` premise is needed, since built-ins range over
; reachable situations only (§12.3).
;
; Use it last: it is quadratic, and nothing else here needs it. To ask "can S
; reach an F", use §7's `can-reach`, which walks backwards in one pass.
(relation reach (Situation Situation))
(rule (reach S S) (situation S))
(rule (reach S T) (edge E S M) (reach M T))

; 2. Isomorphism — the maximal subgroupoid
;
; Situations that reach each other are isomorphic; `phase` names the classes
; by their least-indexed member. Within a class nothing has been spent.
;
; `mutual` lists the equivalence as pairs. The first literal generates each
; situation's phase and the second only that phase's members, so the cost is
; the answer's size.
(relation mutual (Situation Situation))
(rule (mutual S T) (phase S P) (phase T P))

; `recurrent S`: S lies on a cycle. Read off an edge that stays inside the
; phase, since atoms cannot be compared for inequality. Non-termination
; questions take this shape: a recurrent situation where the goal fails.
(relation recurrent (Situation))
(rule (recurrent S) (edge E S T) (phase S P) (phase T P))

; 3. The posetal reflection — what a move costs
;
; Quotienting by §2 leaves a poset, the `phase-step` graph, acyclic by
; construction. `before` gives the whole trap structure without naming a goal.
;
; `phase-before` orders phases; `before` reads it back onto situations. The
; phase pair is bound first so each situation pair is generated once.
(relation phase-before (Situation Situation))
(rule (phase-before P Q) (phase-step P Q))
(rule (phase-before P R) (phase-step P Q) (phase-before Q R))

(relation before (Situation Situation))
(rule (before S T) (phase-before P Q) (phase S P) (phase T Q))

; 4. One-way doors — irreversibility, per move
;
; A named move and the situation it leaves for good. A move is one-way exactly
; when it crosses out of its phase. A model with no rows is fully reversible.
(relation one-way (Edge Situation Situation))
(rule (one-way E S T) (edge E S T) (phase S P) (phase T Q) (phase-step P Q))

; 5. Final phases — where a model ends up
;
; A situation in a terminal class of §3's poset: it may still move, but
; everything it reaches reaches it back.
;
; `escapes` exists because a variable inside `not` must be bound elsewhere
; (range restriction), so "no later phase" must be a relation first.
(relation escapes (Situation))
(rule (escapes S) (phase S P) (phase-step P Q))
(relation final-phase (Situation))
(rule (final-phase S) (situation S) (not (escapes S)))

; 6. Dead ends
;
; A situation with no move at all. §15 reports these too; as a relation they
; can be joined, e.g. with `one-way`.
(relation moves (Situation))
(rule (moves S) (edge E S T))
(relation dead-end (Situation))
(rule (dead-end S) (situation S) (not (moves S)))

; 7. Goals — the modalities, as three composable lines
;
; A guard arrives as a form slot. The river crossing:
;
;   (satisfies intact     (and (is farmer.at right) (is wolf.at right)
;                              (is goat.at right)   (is cabbage.at right)))
;   (can-reach  finishable intact)
;   (trapped    blunders   finishable)
;
; Polarity:
;
;   possible F   `satisfies` IS the satisfying set     non-empty ⟺ holds
;   never F      `satisfies` of the FORBIDDEN guard    empty     ⟺ holds
;   live F       `trapped` is the COUNTEREXAMPLE set   empty     ⟺ holds
;
; `trapped` names the complement so a `live` answer is not misread as a
; satisfying set. Three forms rather than one because a form cannot invent
; names for intermediates.
;
; `can-reach` walks backward from the goal set, one pass over the edges.
(form (satisfies R G)
  (relation R 1)
  (rule (R S) (situation S) (holds S G)))

(form (can-reach C R)
  (relation C 1)
  (rule (C S) (R S))
  (rule (C S) (edge E S T) (C T)))

(form (trapped X C)
  (relation X 1)
  (rule (X S) (situation S) (not (C S))))

; 8. Runs that never arrive — `inevitable`, the other way round
;
; The counterexample set of `inevitable G`, computed independently of the
; engine, so the two can be cross-checked. A run avoids G by circling for ever
; or by stopping short; `escaping` has a rule for each. `inevitable G` holds
; iff it is empty.
;
;   (satisfies decided (all (p participant)
;                        (or (is p.state committed) (is p.state aborted))))
;   (off-set   undecided decided)
;   (stays     waiting   undecided)
;   (escaping  forever   undecided waiting)
;
; Fairness clauses are not modelled here: this is the plain modality. `stays`
; is a quadratic closure; use these forms to check the engine, not replace it.
(form (off-set O R)
  (relation O 1)
  (rule (O S) (situation S) (not (R S))))

(form (stays U O)
  (relation U 2)
  (rule (U S S) (O S))
  (rule (U S T) (O S) (edge E S M) (U M T)))

(form (escaping X O U)
  (relation X 1)
  (rule (X S) (O S) (edge E S M) (U M S))
  (rule (X S) (O S) (not (moves S))))

; Not here: arrow properties such as injectivity. A form substitutes only a
; path's root (`Expander.subst`), so no template can be generic over an arrow;
; write one rule per arrow in the model's own .rules file. Such questions can
; never be laws: §10.2 cannot compare two bound variables in a guard.
